academy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fiat%2FS-taSAckJ-HZn407K8o5p1rYlzBDAIy_7DDW2z7TJZE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fiat%252FQONSEk87zqRm50Uzhk2ZpGMslzYOWTkJ_ocCRmTdCic.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fiat%2FQONSEk87zqRm50Uzhk2ZpGMslzYOWTkJ_ocCRmTdCic.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fiat%2FQONSEk87zqRm50Uzhk2ZpGMslzYOWTkJ_ocCRmTdCic.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>"],"answer":2,"solution":"No solution available. ","difficulty":"Medium","source":"IAT (IISER) 2024","_marksId":"67fdff73378e1624ea7b6b9c","answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect"},{"id":23960,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Amines","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"What is the order of $\\text{p}K_b$ for the following molecules in an aqueous medium?<br>","options":["$\\textbf{S} \\lt  \\textbf{P} \\lt  \\textbf{R} \\lt  \\textbf{Q}$","$\\textbf{S} \\lt  \\textbf{P} \\lt  \\textbf{Q} \\lt  \\textbf{R}$","$\\textbf{P} \\lt  \\textbf{R} \\lt  \\textbf{Q} \\lt  \\textbf{S}$","$\\textbf{P} \\lt  \\textbf{Q} \\lt  \\textbf{R} \\lt  \\textbf{S}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"The basicity of an amine is inversely proportional to its $\\text{p}K_b$ value. A stronger base has a lower $\\text{p}K_b$.<br><br>1. Molecule P (Benzylamine): It is an aliphatic amine. The lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen atom is localized and not involved in resonance with the benzene ring. Therefore, it is the strongest base among the given compounds and has the lowest $\\text{p}K_b$.<br><br>2. Molecules Q, R, and S: These are aromatic amines where the nitrogen atom is directly attached to the benzene ring. The lone pair on nitrogen is delocalized into the ring via resonance, making them weaker bases than P.<br><br>3. Comparing Q, R, and S:<br>- S (Aniline): The lone pair is delocalized into the ring. It is the weakest base among the four.<br>- Q (N-Methylaniline): The presence of one electron-donating methyl group (+I effect) increases the electron density on the nitrogen atom, making it a stronger base than aniline.<br>- R (N,N-Dimethylaniline): It has two methyl groups, providing a greater +I effect. Furthermore, the bulky $-\\text{N}(\\text{CH}_3)_2$ group experiences steric repulsion from the ortho-hydrogens of the benzene ring. This causes steric inhibition of resonance (SIR), twisting the amino group out of the plane of the ring. As a result, the delocalization of the nitrogen lone pair into the ring is significantly reduced, making the lone pair more available for protonation. Thus, R is a stronger base than Q.<br><br>The overall basicity order is: $\\textbf{P} \\gt  \\textbf{R} \\gt  \\textbf{Q} \\gt  \\textbf{S}$.<br><br>Consequently, the order of $\\text{p}K_b$ values is the reverse of the basicity order:<br>$\\textbf{P} \\lt  \\textbf{R} \\lt  \\textbf{Q} \\lt  \\textbf{S}$.<br><br>Answer: $\\textbf{P} \\lt  \\textbf{R} \\lt  \\textbf{Q} \\lt  \\textbf{S}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"IAT (IISER) 2026","_marksId":"6a4521838c20d3b91d64f969"},{"id":23961,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Amines","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"Benzamide is treated with $\\mathrm{Br}_2$ and $\\mathrm{NaOH}(\\mathrm{aq})$ to form the product X , which is then reacted with $\\mathrm{NaNO}_2$ and $\\mathrm{HCl}(\\mathrm{aq})$ at $0-5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ to form Y . Y is immediately treated with ethanol to give $\\mathbf{Z}$. What is $\\mathbf{Z}$ ?","options":["<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage